---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { MDNA } from "../../data/compare";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";
import { compMatrix, compPicker, deepMatrix } from "../../lib/matrix";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";
---

<BaseLayout route="compare/hub">
  <PageIntro
    route={"compare/hub"}
    tpl={"T6"}
    crumb={"Compare"}
    section={false}
    title={"Compare Mersive Polaris with anyone."}
  />
  <p class="dek">
    ClickShare, Airtame, ScreenBeam, Microsoft Teams Rooms. Tick any combination, or all of them.
    <em>(Placeholder data pending the canonical matrix.)</em> &nbsp;<a href="/platform/tco"
      >What does the difference cost? The 10-year TCO calculator →</a
    >
  </p>
    <p class="note">Every Polaris hardware value in the matrix &mdash; ports, silicon, the 10-year platform lifecycle and the invariants it holds constant &mdash; is documented once, on <a href="/products/pro/spec#platform-lifecycle">the Pro spec sheet</a>, and is not restated here. Competitor cells keep their own published sources.</p>

  <div class="taxq">
    <b>The grid is ours on purpose.</b> These rows aren't a feature checklist. They're the two questions
    that sort the market: <b>where does the meeting live</b> (your device → your device + the room's AV
    → hosted BY the room) and <b>is the room platform-locked or agnostic</b>, then security, silicon,
    and lifecycle. Ask any vendor these questions in this order, then ask what each answer costs at 500 rooms, across three networks, and the moment a guest walks in with a Chromebook.
    <a href="/platform/taxonomy">See where meetings live →</a>
  </div>

  {/* Both matrices are rendered HERE, at build time, by src/lib/matrix.ts — the
      same function that renders the Trust Center's slice. Until 15 Aug 2026 they
      were built in the browser into two empty divs, so this page served 0 <td>
      cells and 0 source links while grading fourteen vendors on whether THEIR
      evidence is retrievable. The picker below hides columns with a class and
      never removes them: script off gets every column and every citation. */}
  <Fragment set:html={compPicker()} />

  {/* The outline used to run H1 → H3 here and no guard caught it, because the
      "Our take" panel was written by script after the page loaded. Making the
      matrix static made the skip real and check-outline.py found it the same
      minute — a small argument for putting things in the HTML. */}
  <h2 class="sr-only">The comparison matrix: 27 questions across 14 vendors</h2>

  <Fragment set:html={compMatrix()} /><p class="note">Vivi is graded partial on warranty because Vivi contradicts itself: its FAQ says hardware replacement is covered, while its pricing update states that &ldquo;Vivi hardware replacements purchased on or after July 1, 2026 will be available at a one-time cost of $129.&rdquo;</p>
  {/* "Our take" across the whole field is the default selection, so it is served
      as HTML. The script rewrites it only when a reader narrows to one brand. */}
  <div id="mdna">
    <div class="mdna reveal vis">
      <h3>Our take</h3>
      <p set:html={MDNA._all.why} />
      <div class="concede" set:html={MDNA._all.concede} />
    </div>
  </div>

  <Fragment set:html={deepMatrix()} />
  <p class="note">
    Published single-display source ceilings: Kramer Campus&sup2; 6 &middot; Crestron 4 &middot;
    WolfVision 4 &middot; ScreenBeam 4 &middot; Extron 4 &middot; BenQ 4 &middot; Vivi 4 &middot;
    Yealink 4 &middot; DisplayNote 4, or 9 in Extended Gridview &middot; Barco 2, and only by
    consuming two physical displays &middot; Cisco 1 &middot; Airtame not published. Several carry
    conditions the spec line does not show: Crestron&rsquo;s Canvas layout is automatic with no user
    control and one factory system mode pins the limit to 1; ScreenBeam&rsquo;s Multi-View is
    unavailable during a BYOM session; DisplayNote&rsquo;s Grid View requires Moderator Mode;
    Vivi&rsquo;s split screen is unavailable in Multi-Display and Combined Rooms. [Workspace/silicon
    rows describe Essentials and Pro; Launch derates. Campus PA/alerts is Vivi&rsquo;s, not ours.]
  </p>

  <div class="two">
    <div class="card">
      <h2 class="sr-only">Where to start comparing</h2>
      <h3>The bigger question</h3>
      <p>Vendor vs vendor matters less than which cell of the market you buy into.</p>
      <a href="/platform/taxonomy">See where meetings live →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Considering a VTC room system?</h3>
      <p>
        MTR, Zoom Rooms: a per-room platform license, one vendor by default, and a design where the default motion is start-a-call just to
        share, so heads drop into laptops.
      </p>
      <a href="/compare/mtr">Why not a VTC room system? →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Still handing out dongles?</h3>
      <p>Buttons and sticks replaced the cable. The meeting moved on.</p>
      <a href="/compare/dongles">Why not a dongle? →</a>
    </div>
    {/* /compare/barco and /compare/airtame were reachable from nowhere at all —
        no nav entry and no link in any page's body copy, so nothing on the site
        pointed at them and nothing ever would have. They are siblings of the two
        cards above and belong in the same group. Card copy stays neutral and
        makes no comparative claim: the head-to-head pages carry the sourced
        detail, and the claims on them are still under review. */}
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Comparing against Barco ClickShare?</h3>
      <p>The incumbent in a lot of rooms. Where it is strong, and where the model differs.</p>
      <a href="/compare/barco">Polaris vs Barco ClickShare →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Comparing against Airtame?</h3>
      <p>Strong on signage and simplicity. Where the two overlap, and where they do not.</p>
      <a href="/compare/airtame">Polaris vs Airtame →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <CtaBand />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import "../../scripts/pages/compare";
</script>
